/// The 802.11 station's configuration (`wlan`, 550-NET-0018): the networks around, joining one, the link, and the
/// networks it remembers. Agreed with the tools track for `wifi` (550-APP-0033), from its proposal in requests-NET.md.
///
/// The station's endpoint serves two interfaces, told apart by the client's badge (`mind::network`): a client with
/// `BADGE_WIFI` set speaks this one, any other `idl/net.wit` (the network stack's card). With `BADGE_WIFI_CHANGE` a
/// client may join, leave and forget; with `BADGE_WIFI` alone it only reads (`denied`).
///
/// An SSID is up to 32 bytes as the access point sends them: most are UTF-8, some are not (GBK, Latin-1), so they travel
/// as bytes and a program shows them as it can. An empty SSID is a hidden network's. The station does no DHCP: the
/// address the link got is the network stack's (`ip`).
///
/// 1.1 (550-NET-0035, for 550-APP-0082): the country the station runs under, from the regulatory catalogue built into
/// it (wireless-regdb at a pinned commit, named by `catalogue`). A country is asked for by its two-letter code and kept
/// by `keystore` (keystore.wit 1.3), never assumed: with none applied the station probes nowhere and `join` answers
/// `unsupported`. The radio applies the country's limits under its own (wlanhw.wit 1.2, proposed). Asking for a
/// country leaves the network and drops the last scan, so nothing chosen under the old rules is used under the new
/// ones. A station built without a catalogue answers the country calls `unsupported` and keeps 1.0's behaviour
/// (550-NET-0035, stage A).
package mind:wifi@1.1.0;

interface wifi {
    /// How a network is protected: `wpa2` is RSN with PSK and CCMP, which the station joins, as it joins `open`;
    /// `other` is any other RSN (TKIP, management frame protection required, another AKM).
    enum security { open, wep, wpa2, wpa3, enterprise, other }
    /// `no-device`: no radio; `busy`: a scan is under way; `invalid`: a passphrase outside the rules (WPA2-PSK: 8 to 63
    /// printable ASCII characters, or 64 hexadecimal digits) or none for a WPA2 network; `denied`: the client may only
    /// read; `unsupported`: the network's protection is one the station does not join, or remembering is not available;
    /// `not-found`: no network of that name in the last scan.
    enum error { no-device, busy, invalid, denied, unsupported, not-found }
    enum state { off, scanning, joining, joined, failed }
    /// Why joining failed or the link was lost: `wrong-passphrase` (the handshake was answered and never completed),
    /// `no-answer`, `refused` (authentication, association, or an RSN element in the handshake other than the advertised
    /// one), `not-found`, `unsupported` (a protection the station does not join, or an access point that refused it for
    /// lacking one of its basic rates), `lost` (deauthenticated or silent; the station joins again by itself).
    enum reason { none, wrong-passphrase, no-answer, refused, not-found, unsupported, lost }

    /// An access point the last scan heard: its network's name, BSSID (low 48 bits, first byte in bits 40-47), channel,
    /// signal as minus dBm (48: -48 dBm; 0 unknown), protection, and whether the station remembers a key for the name.
    record network { ssid: list<u8, 32>, bssid: u64, channel: u8, weakness: u8, security: security, remembered: bool }
    /// The link: its state, the network and access point joined or being joined, the signal, the data rate in 500 kb/s
    /// units (0 before joining), and the reason of the last failure or loss.
    record status { state: state, ssid: list<u8, 32>, bssid: u64, channel: u8, weakness: u8, rate: u8, reason: reason }

    /// Starts a scan of every channel and answers when it ends (about 2 s), with the access points heard. While joining
    /// or joined it does not leave the channel and answers the list as it stands.
    scan: func() -> result<list<network, 32>, error>;
    /// Starts joining the strongest access point of `ssid` from the last scan (`status` follows). `passphrase` empty for
    /// an open network or one the station remembers. `remember` has `keystore` keep the network's key (550-NET-0024): it
    /// alone holds it, and the station gets each handshake's keys from it (MC-11.9); `unsupported` when it cannot. The
    /// passphrase is never answered back. Change badge only.
    join: func(ssid: list<u8, 32>, passphrase: string<64>, remember: bool) -> result<_, error>;
    status: func() -> status;
    /// Leaves the network (change badge only).
    leave: func() -> result<_, error>;
    /// Forgets a remembered network; false when none had the name (change badge only).
    forget: func(ssid: list<u8, 32>) -> result<bool, error>;

    /// A channel under a country (1.1): `send`, the station may probe and join there; `listen`, the rules forbid
    /// initiating (NO-IR) or ask for radar detection (DFS), so the station only listens there and joins an access point
    /// it heard; `barred`, the rules do not cover it, or restrict it in a way the station does not keep (indoor or
    /// outdoor only, one modulation alone, point-to-point); `radio`, the rules allow it and the radio does not.
    enum standing { send, listen, barred, radio }
    /// One of the radio's channels under a country: its number, its standing, and the most power the rules allow there
    /// (EIRP in dBm, rounded down; 0 when barred).
    record channel { number: u8, standing: standing, eirp: u8 }
    /// A country of the catalogue: its code (two capital letters, as wireless-regdb has them), and how many of the
    /// radio's channels it lets the station send on, and only listen on.
    record country { code: string<2>, send: u8, listen: u8 }
    /// A page of the catalogue: how many countries it has, and up to 32 of them from the index asked for, by code. The
    /// world domain `00` is not one of them: it is never a chosen country.
    record countries { total: u16, page: list<country, 32> }
    /// `none`: no country asked for, or none kept; `applied`: the station runs under `applied`; `failed`: the radio
    /// refused `asked`'s limits, and nothing is applied (the station does not fall back to an earlier country).
    enum country-state { none, applied, failed }
    /// The station's country: the code asked for and the code applied (empty: none), the state, whether the radio
    /// confirmed its limits (a wlanhw.wit 1.1 radio cannot: the station alone keeps to the channels), whether `keystore`
    /// keeps the choice across restarts, and the catalogue's identity (the first 16 hex digits of the SHA-256 of its
    /// db.txt; empty: no catalogue).
    record country-status { asked: string<2>, applied: string<2>, state: country-state, radio: bool, kept: bool, catalogue: string<16> }

    /// A page of the catalogue from index `from` (1.1). `unsupported`: no catalogue in this station.
    countries: func(from: u16) -> result<countries, error>;
    /// The radio's channels under country `code` as the station would use them, `00` too, for information (1.1).
    /// `invalid`: not two capital letters, nor `00`; `not-found`: not in the catalogue; `no-device`: no radio yet;
    /// `unsupported`: no catalogue in this station.
    channels: func(code: string<2>) -> result<list<channel, 64>, error>;
    /// The station's country (1.1).
    country: func() -> country-status;
    /// Asks for country `code` (change badge only, 1.1): the station leaves the network and drops the last scan, has the
    /// radio apply the country's limits and `keystore` keep the code, and answers the status after. `invalid`: not two
    /// capital letters, or `00`; `not-found`: not in the catalogue; `no-device`: no radio yet; `unsupported`: no
    /// catalogue in this station. A radio that refuses the limits is not an error: the status says `failed`.
    set-country: func(code: string<2>) -> result<country-status, error>;
}
